==Aftermath==
{{Wikinews|Portugal overcome Netherlands 1-0 on way to quarter-final}}
{{external media
| float  = right
| video1 = [https://www.youtube.com/live/dGS030bD4A0?si=V58bjkA29OFIj-ZU Battle of Nuremberg], YouTube video by FIFA
}}
In the aftermath of the match, referee Ivanov was criticised by [[FIFA]] president [[Sepp Blatter]], who suggested that Ivanov should have given himself a yellow card for his poor performance during the match.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/5116414.stm|title=Blatter criticises referee Ivanov |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=26 June 2006 |accessdate=26 June 2006}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.eurosport.com/football/worldcup/2006/blatter-blast-for-ivanov_sto914102/story.shtml |title=Blatter blast for Ivanov |work=[[Eurosport]] |date=25 June 2006 |accessdate=14 June 2021}}</ref> Blatter later regretted these words and promised to apologise officially.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.mykhel.com/football/blatter-regrets-criticism-of-referee-ivanov-019624.html|title=Blatter regrets criticism of referee Ivanov|work=OneIndia News|date=4 July 2006|access-date=26 December 2012|archive-date=8 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220408083304/https://www.mykhel.com/football/blatter-regrets-criticism-of-referee-ivanov-019624.html|url-status=live}}</ref> FIFA announced that Ivanov would referee no further matches in the tournament.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://edition.cnn.com/2006/SPORT/football/06/28/world.referees/index.html |title=Under-fire refs get World Cup boot |date=29 June 2006 |access-date=14 June 2021 |work=CNN}}</ref> Ivanov's father [[Valentin Ivanov (footballer, born 1934)|Valentin Ivanov]] defended his son, saying it had been FIFA's demand for referees to be tough on the players.<ref>{{cite news |last=Culf |first=Andrew |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2006/jun/27/worldcup2006.football |title=Red cards and recriminations: have refs gone too far this time? |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=27 June 2006 |accessdate=14 June 2021}}</ref>

When Portugal faced [[England national football team|England]] in the subsequent quarter-final, they were without the suspended Deco and Costinha. Portugal managed to reach the semi-finals. However, they had two players suspended for that match due to accumulated yellow cards, including the ones against the Netherlands.

The record for yellow cards in a World Cup game was surpassed in [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022]], when [[Antonio Mateu Lahoz]] issued a total of 18 yellows (16 for players, one sent off for two yellow cards and 2 more to coaching staff) during the quarter-final match between [[Battle of Lusail|the Netherlands and Argentina]]. However, the record was broken during extra time, meaning that the record still stood for most cards shown in 90 minutes of a World Cup match.

